# YAML

Write clear, valid, reviewable YAML from the first draft — not a monolith blob
to clean up later. Domain-specific files still follow this rule, then their
own craft:

| Kind | Extra rule |
| --- | --- |
| Kubernetes manifests | `code-kubernetes` |
| Terraform / HCL | `code-terraform` (not YAML) |
| CI workflows | `code-ci-from-main` when editing pipeline YAML |

## Style & structure

- Prefer `.yaml` or match the project’s existing extension (`.yml` vs
  `.yaml`); don’t mix both for the same kind of file without reason
- Use 2-space indentation (unless the project standard differs); never tabs
- Quote strings when they can be misread as booleans/numbers/null (`"on"`,
  `"off"`, `"yes"`, `"no"`, versions like `"1.10"`, bare `*` paths, etc.)
- Prefer block style for nested/structured data; use flow style (`{ }` /
  `[ ]`) only for short, simple inline values
- Keep related keys together; order for readers consistently within a file
  type (see domain rules for required key order)
- Prefer several focused files or clear top-level sections over one huge
  undifferentiated document when the tool allows it
- Be reasonable: don’t split a 15-line compose service into micro-files

## Multi-document streams

- Use `---` only when the consumer expects a multi-doc stream (Kubernetes
  manifests, some Helm/Kustomize inputs) or the project already does
- One logical resource / document per `---` section; do not jam unrelated
  resources into one section
- Do not put comments *between* documents in a way that breaks parsers that
  treat the stream strictly — prefer a short header comment above the first
  doc or inside each doc

## Docs & comments

Document purpose; comment intent — never narrate obvious keys. Follow
`code-comments`.

- Comment non-obvious values, coupling, or “do not change without …” caveats
  only — skip obligatory headers when keys already make purpose clear

```yaml
# compose.yaml — local API + DB for development (volume-mounted app code).

services:
  api:
    # Bind-mount source for fast iteration; image rebuild not required for app edits.
    volumes:
      - ./app:/app
```

## Correctness & safety

- Produce valid YAML: consistent indentation, no tab/space mix, correct list
  vs map nesting
- Prefer explicit types via quoting or schema when ambiguous; avoid relying
  on YAML’s implicit typing surprises
- Never commit secrets, tokens, or private keys in YAML — use env vars,
  secret refs, or a secret store
- Prefer `${ENV}` / secret refs / external secret objects over hard-coded
  credentials
- When editing generated or schema-bound YAML (CRDs, Helm values, Actions),
  match the expected schema and existing project conventions
- Keep anchors (`&` / `*`) only when they reduce real repetition — don’t
  obscure readability with clever alias graphs
- Prefer `null` / omitting optional keys over empty-string placeholders that
  the schema treats as set

## Anti-patterns

```yaml
# ❌ BAD — unquoted ambiguous values, secret inlined, no context
enabled: yes
replicas: 01
password: s3cr3t

# ✅ GOOD — quoted when needed, secret from env, brief intent comment
# Feature gate for the beta API path.
enabled: "yes"
replicas: 1
password: "${DB_PASSWORD}"
```
